/*! \page developer_build_source_page Source List (sources.mk) Files

\ref developer_build_config_page (Prev) &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
\ref developer_build_dependency_page  (Next)
<BR>

Almost every subdirectory of the  src/ directory tree contain a source
list file named "sources.mk", which we refer to as a source list file. 
This file is a makefile fragment that is included into other makefiles. 
The primary purpose of each such file is to maintain makefile variables
that contain lists of the object files and dependency files that will
be created by compiling the source files in that directory. The source 
list file in each namespace-level directories also define a makefile 
rule for constructing an associated static library.

\section developer_build_list_sources_sec Source file lists

Every sources.mk source list file that contains compilable \*.cpp C++ 
source contains a makefile variable whose value is a list of all of 
the \*.cpp source files that are located in that directory.  The name 
of that variable is always a string of the form
\verbatim
[directory]_CPP
\endverbatim
in which the symbol "[directory]" represents a mangled form of the 
name of the subdirectory of src that contains the source list file.  
Specifically, the [directory] string in each such variable name is 
constructed by taking the relative path from the src/ directory to a 
subdirectory of interest and replacing each "/" directory separator 
in this path an underscore ("_").  As an example, the file 
src/pscf/chem/sources.mk defines a variable named
\verbatim
pscf_chem_CPP
\endverbatim
Here, the directory path pscf/chem defined relative to the src directory
has been converted into the variable name pscf_chem_ . The value of this
variable is a list of paths to all compilable source files that are located
in the directory tree rooted at the directory that contains this sources.mk
file. Each path in this list must be given as a relative path, defined
relative to the root of the src directory tree.

Each directory that contain \*.cu CUDA C++ files also contains an analogous 
variable whose value is a list of all of the \*.cu source files in that 
directory and all nested subdirectories. The name of this variable is a 
string of the form
\verbatim
[directory]_CU
\endverbatim
in which "[directory]" denotes a mangled form fo the directory name of
the same form as that used to construct the [directory]_CPP variable.

If a directory with the src directory tree contains both standard \*.cpp 
C++ files and \*.cu CUDA C++ files, then the source file list in that 
directory will define two different makefile variables, with suffixes 
_CPP and _CU, that contain lists of these two different types of file. 

Source files lists are not directly used by the build system, but are 
used simply as inputs to text manipulation commands that use them to
build object and dependency file lists of the types discussed below.

\section developer_build_list_objdep_sec Object file lists

The sources.mk file in each source file directory also defines a single
makefile variable with a name of the form 
\verbatim
[directory]_OBJS 
\endverbatim
in which [directory]_OBJS is a mangled form of the path to the 
directory.  For example, the file src/pscf/chem/sources.mk defines a 
variable named
\verbatim
pscf_chem_OBJS
\endverbatim
The value of this variable contains a list of paths to corresponding 
object files that are created by compiling the source files contained
in this directory and in any nested subdirectories that it contains.
In what follows, we refer to the [directory]_OBJS file in each directory 
as an object file list.

Every path in an object file list must be an absolute path to a file
in the build directory, defined relative to the root of the entire 
file system. Note that this is different from the convention used for 
source file lists, in which the path to each file is a relative path, 
defined relative to the root of the src directory tree. 

The object file is in each directory is created automatically by 
applying some simple text manipulation operations to the source file 
lists. In a directory that contains source files but contains no
subdirectories, the object file list is constructed from corresponding
source files lists by operations that prepend absolute path to the
build directory to each relative path a source file list and changing
the file name suffix from .cpp or .cu to .o or .ou. Because it is
automatically generated, the object file list thus does not need to
be manually modified by a developer when a source file is added to 
or removed from a directory. Developers are only responsbile for
maintaining source file lists.

If a directory contains both standard \*.cpp C++ files and \*.cu CUDA 
C++ files, then the object file list will contain object files created 
by compiling both types of source file. Object files created by 
compiling these different types of source files are created by different 
pattern rules, using different compilers, and have different file name 
suffixes:

  - Names of object files created by compiling standard \*.cpp C++ files 
    end with a suffix .o,  

  - Names of object files that are created by compiling \*.cu CUDA C++ 
    files end with a suffix .ou. 

Each of the object files listed in an object file list is a target of 
an associated makefile rule. The list of pre-requisites of each such 
target is contained in an associated dependency file, as discussed 
below.

\section developer_build_list_dep_sec Dependency file lists

In addition to source file and object file lists, the source file in 
each source file directory defines a single make variable with a name 
of the file
\verbatim
[directory]_DEPS 
\endverbatim
This variable, which we refer to as a dependency file list, contains
a list of absolute paths to dependency files that are created by
creating all of the source files in the relevant directory and its
subdirectories. 

The paths in a dependency file list are absolute paths to files that
are created in the build directory. Each dependency file list is 
created automatically by applying text manipulation functions to the 
relevant source file list or lists.

If a directory contains both \*.cpp C++ source files and \*.cu CUDA 
C++ files, then the [directory]_DEPS variable will contain names of 
dependency files created by compiling both types of source file, 
which have different file name suffixes:

  - The name of a dependency file that is created as a side effect
    of the rule that compiles a standard \*.cpp C++ files end with a 
    suffix .d

  - Names of a dependency file that is created as a side effect of 
    the rule that compiles \*.cu CUDA C++ files end with a suffix 
    .du

Each dependency file contains a makefile rule in which the target is 
the path to the associated object file.  The pre-requisites of each 
object file target include all of the \*.h and \*.tpp files in the 
src directory that are directly or indirectly included by the source 
file that is compiled to create the object file target. The dependency 
file associated with each source and object file is generated by 
the build system by using the compiler to generate a list of these
included files. 

\section developer_build_list_examples_sec Examples

In the remainder of this page, we discuss examples of sources.mk source 
list files located at three different levels of the src directory tree:

   - Directories that do not contain any enclosed subdirectories, 
     and that are not namespace-level directories.

   - Directories that contain enclosed subdirectories, but that are not 
     namespace-level directories.

   - Namepace-level subdirectories of the src directory

\subsection developer_build_list_example1_sub Directories with no enclosed subdirectories

The lowest level type of source list files occur in directories that 
contain source files, but do not contain any enclosed subdirectories, and 
that are not namespace-level directories. In what follows, we display and 
discuss two examples of such source list files. The first example 
examines the source list file in a subdirectory of the src/pscf directory 
that contains only standard \*.cpp C++ source files.  The second example
shows the sources.mk for a subdirectory of the src/rp directory that 
contains both \*.cpp and \*.cu files. This file also contains with some 
logic to control conditional compilation of different types of source 
file.

<b> Example 1 </b>: As a first example, the contents of the file 
src/pscf/chem/sources.mk is shown below. This is a directory with no
enclosed subdirectories that contains \*.cpp C++ files, but no \*.cu 
CUDA C++ files.
\verbatim
pscf_chem_CPP= \
  pscf/chem/Monomer.cpp \
  pscf/chem/Vertex.cpp \
  pscf/chem/Edge.cpp \
  pscf/chem/Species.cpp \
  pscf/chem/SolventSpecies.cpp \
  pscf/chem/PolymerSpecies.cpp \
  pscf/chem/PolymerType.cpp \
  pscf/chem/PolymerModel.cpp \
  pscf/chem/Composition.cpp \
  pscf/chem/Deby.cpp \
  pscf/chem/VertexIterator.cpp \
  pscf/chem/EdgeIterator.cpp

pscf_chem_OBJS=\
     $(addprefix $(BLD_DIR)/, $(pscf_chem_CPP:.cpp=.o))

pscf_chem_DEPS=\
     $(addprefix $(BLD_DIR)/, $(pscf_chem_CPP:.cpp=.d))

\endverbatim
The value assigned to pscf_chem_CPP in this example uses a makefile syntax
in which a backslash (\\) character is used to continue a statement to the
next line. Lines 2 - 12 of this example thus assign a value to pscf_chem
that is simply a list of file paths separated by spaces.

The value assigned to pscf_chem_CPP in this example is a list of all of 
the compilable \*.cpp files in the relevant subdirectory, src/pscf/chem.  
All paths in a source file list must be given as relative paths, defined 
relative to the src/ directory, as done in this example.

The object file list variable pscf_chem_OBJS and dependency file list
pscf_CHEM_DEPS are constructed in the last few lines of this example by 
applying some standard text manipulation functions.

Consider the command that assigns a value to the object list variable
pscf_chem_OBJS.  The value that is assigned to this variable is a list 
of absolute paths for object files, with filename suffix \*.o.  This 
list is constructed here by applying two makefile text manipulation 
functions to the value of source file list pscf_chem_CPP. First, the 
":" substitution operator is used in the expression 
\code
  $(pscf_chem_:.cpp=.o) 
\endcode 
to replace the .cpp suffix with a .o suffix in each of the relative 
paths in \$(pscf_chem_).  Then, the built in "addprefix" function is 
used to add the value $(BLD_DIR)/ as a common prefix to every name 
in the list. Here, $(BLD_DIR) is the absolute path to the build 
directory, which is defined in the relevant config.mk configuration 
file. These manipulations yield a list of absolute paths to all of 
the \*.o object files that should be created by compiling the files 
in the source file list.

<b> Example 2 </b>: As a second example, we consider the contents 
of the file src/rp/fts/brownian/sources.mk, which is located in a
lower-level subdirectory of the src/rp directory tree.

The src/rp directory is somewhat special in that it contains both 
standard C++ \*.cpp files that are used to construct the pscf_rpc 
program and closely analogous \*.cu CUDA C++ files that are used 
to construct the pscf_rpg program.  Throughout this directory tree, 
\*.cpp and \*.cu source files exist as pairs of files with the 
same base name but different suffixes. For example, the directory
src/rp/fts/brownian contains a pair of files named LMBdStep.cpp 
and LMBdStep.cu, with the same base LMBdStep but different file
name suffixes. Each of the two files in each such pair contains
code that instantiates a different specialization of a common 
class template, in which shared base name is the name of the 
class template. These two different specializations of each such
class template are designed to use different hardware.

For concreteness, consider the the two files named LMBdStep.cpp 
and LMBdStep.cu in directory src/rp/fts/brownian. These two files 
instantiate two different specializations of a class template 
named LMBdStep. The implementation of this class template, which 
is define in a file named LMBdStep.tpp, defines an algorithm for
the Leimkuhler-Matthews Brownian dynamics step algorithm used in 
PS-FTS simulations. The file LMBDStep.cpp, which is compiled by 
a standard C++ compiler, instantiates a specialization of a the 
LMBDStep class template that is designed to be used as part of 
the pscf_rpc program, and to use standard CPU hardware.  The file 
BdStep.cu instead instantiates a specialization of the LMBDStep 
template that is designed to be used as part of the pscf_rpg 
program, and that is designed to use a GPU.

Conditional compilation of the \*.cpp and \*.cu files in the src/rp 
directory tree is controlled by the makefile variables PSCF_CPP and 
PSCF_CUDA that are introduced in the relevant config.mk configuration
file. These two variables also control construction of the pscf_rpc 
and and pscf_rpg programs, respectively.  The \*.cu files in the 
src/rp directory tree are all compiled if and only if PSCF_CUDA is 
defined, thus enabling construction of the pscf_rpg program. The 
\*.cpp files in this directory tree are all compiled if and only 
if PSCF_CPP is defined, thus enabling construction of the pscf_rpc 
program.  Understanding of this logic is necessary to understand the 
structure of the source list files in this directory tree.

The content of the file src/rp/fts/brownian/sources.mk is shown
below as an example:
\verbatim
rp_fts_brownian_OBJS=
rp_fts_brownian_DEPS=

ifdef PSCF_CPP
  rp_fts_brownian_CPP= \
    rp/fts/brownian/BdStep.cpp \
    rp/fts/brownian/BdSimulator.cpp \
    rp/fts/brownian/ExplicitBdStep.cpp \
    rp/fts/brownian/PredCorrBdStep.cpp \
    rp/fts/brownian/LMBdStep.cpp \
    rp/fts/brownian/BdStepFactory.cpp
  rp_fts_brownian_OBJS+=\
       $(addprefix $(BLD_DIR)/, $(rp_fts_brownian_CPP:.cpp=.o))
  rp_fts_brownian_DEPS+=\
       $(addprefix $(BLD_DIR)/, $(rp_fts_brownian_CPP:.cpp=.d))
endif

ifdef PSCF_CUDA
  rp_fts_brownian_CU= \
    rp/fts/brownian/BdStep.cu \
    rp/fts/brownian/BdSimulator.cu \
    rp/fts/brownian/ExplicitBdStep.cu \
    rp/fts/brownian/PredCorrBdStep.cu \
    rp/fts/brownian/LMBdStep.cu \
    rp/fts/brownian/BdStepFactory.cu
  rp_fts_brownian_OBJS+=\
      $(addprefix $(BLD_DIR)/, $(rp_fts_brownian_CU:.cu=.ou))
  rp_fts_brownian_DEPS+=\
      $(addprefix $(BLD_DIR)/, $(rp_fts_brownian_CU:.cu=.du))
endif
\endverbatim
The first two lines of this file initialize the object file list
rp_fts_brownian_OBJS and dependency file lists rp_fts_brownian_DEPS
variables to empty strings. The remainder of the file contains two 
blocks that are each executed only if a make variable or PSCF_CPP 
or PSCF_CUDA is defined. Each of these blocks conditionally 
processes names of a single type of source file.

The block that is executed only if PSCF_CPP is defined creates a 
source list rp_fts_brownian_CPP that contains relative paths to all 
of the \*.cpp C++ files in this directory. This is followed by two 
commands that add the absolute paths to corresponding object and 
dependency files to the object and dependency file lists.

The block that is executed only if PSCF_CUDA is defined instead 
creates a source list rp_fts_brownian_CU that contains relative paths 
to all of the \*.cu CUDA files in this directory. The definition of
this variable is followed within this block by two commands that add 
paths to the corresponding object and dependency files to the object 
and dependency file lists.

Observe that the commands that create lists of object and dependency 
files use different file name suffixes for object and dependency files 
that are created by compiling different types of source files. The 
commands that manipulate the variable rp_fts_brownian_CPP use a suffix 
.o for object files and .d for dependency files.  The corresponding 
commands that manipulate rp_fts_brownian_CU instead use a suffix .ou
for object files and .du for dependency files.

\subsection developer_build_list_example2_sub Directories with subdirectories (not namespace-level)

We now consider sources.mk file for a subdirectory of src/ that is not 
a namespace-level directory, but that contains one or more enclosed
subdirectories. In this case, the value of the source file list variable
[directory]_OBJS must be a list of paths for all of the source files in 
the directory tree that is rooted at the directory that contains the 
source.mk file. Similarly, the dependency file list [directory]_DEPS
is a list of all associated dependency files.  These two lists are 
created by including the source.mk files in all enclosed subdirectories
and simply concatenating values of variables that are defined in those 
subdirectories.

<b> Example </b>:
Here is the content of the file src/rp/fts//sources.mk in a directory
src/rp/fts that contains several subirectories, but that is not a
namespace-level directory:

\verbatim
# Include source files lists from subdirectories

include $(SRC_DIR)/rp/fts/simulator/sources.mk
include $(SRC_DIR)/rp/fts/compressor/sources.mk
include $(SRC_DIR)/rp/fts/brownian/sources.mk
include $(SRC_DIR)/rp/fts/montecarlo/sources.mk
include $(SRC_DIR)/rp/fts/perturbation/sources.mk
include $(SRC_DIR)/rp/fts/ramp/sources.mk
include $(SRC_DIR)/rp/fts/trajectory/sources.mk
include $(SRC_DIR)/rp/fts/analyzer/sources.mk

# Define object and dependency list variables for rp/fts

rp_fts_OBJS= \
  $(rp_fts_simulator_OBJS) \
  $(rp_fts_compressor_OBJS) \
  $(rp_fts_brownian_OBJS) \
  $(rp_fts_montecarlo_OBJS) \
  $(rp_fts_perturbation_OBJS) \
  $(rp_fts_ramp_OBJS) \
  $(rp_fts_trajectory_OBJS) \
  $(rp_fts_analyzer_OBJS)

rp_fts_DEPS= \
  $(rp_fts_simulator_DEPS) \
  $(rp_fts_compressor_DEPS) \
  $(rp_fts_brownian_DEPS) \
  $(rp_fts_montecarlo_DEPS) \
  $(rp_fts_perturbation_DEPS) \
  $(rp_fts_ramp_DEPS) \
  $(rp_fts_trajectory_DEPS) \
  $(rp_fts_analyzer_DEPS)

\endverbatim
The first section of this file is a set of include directives that
instruct make to include the contents of the sources.mk files from all
subdirectories of src/rp/fts. The make "include" directive is analogous
to the C/C++ \#include preprocessor command, and simply instructs make
to paste the contents of another file into the current file, verbatim.

The next section of this file assigns values to the makefile variables
rp_fts_OBJS and rp_fts_DEPS . These values are created by simply 
concatenating the values of the corresponding _OBJS and _DEPS variables 
defined in all of the enclosed subdirectories.  This yields value for 
rp_fts_OBJS, for example, that is a list of absolute paths to all object 
files that will be created by compiling all of the source files in all 
subdirctories of src/rp/fts.  The corresponding value for rp_fts_DEPS 
is a list of all of the assocated dependency files.

\subsection developer_build_list_example3_sub Namespace-level directories

The source list file in each the namespace-level subdirectory of src
contains a rule for constructing an associated static library, in
addition to code that constructs source and object file lists similar
to that shown above.

<b> Example </b> : Here is the content of the file src/rp/sources.mk
located in the src/rp namespace-level directory

\verbatim
#-----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Include source list files from subdirectories
include $(SRC_DIR)/rp/field/sources.mk
include $(SRC_DIR)/rp/solvers/sources.mk
include $(SRC_DIR)/rp/system/sources.mk
include $(SRC_DIR)/rp/environment/sources.mk
include $(SRC_DIR)/rp/scft/sources.mk
include $(SRC_DIR)/rp/fts/sources.mk

#-----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Object and dependency file list variables for src/rp

rp_OBJS= \
     $(rp_field_OBJS) \
     $(rp_solvers_OBJS) \
     $(rp_system_OBJS) \
     $(rp_environment_OBJS) \
     $(rp_scft_OBJS) \
     $(rp_fts_OBJS) 

rp_DEPS= \
     $(rp_field_DEPS) \
     $(rp_solvers_DEPS) \
     $(rp_system_DEPS) \
     $(rp_environment_DEPS) \
     $(rp_scft_DEPS) \
     $(rp_fts_DEPS)

#-----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Path and makefile target for the rp/librp.a library file

rp_LIBNAME=rp
rp_LIB=$(BLD_DIR)/rp/lib$(rp_LIBNAME).a

$(rp_LIB): $(rp_OBJS)
	$(AR) rcs $(rp_LIB) $(rp_OBJS)

#-----------------------------------------------------------------------
\endverbatim

The first two sections are analogous to the two sections seen in the
previous example. The first section includes the source list files 
located in all subdirectories. The second defines object file and 
dependency file lists by concatenating values defined in sources.mk
files.

The only new feature in this example is the section at the end that 
defines a rule to construct a static library. This type of rule for
defining a static library exists in the sources.mk file located in
each namespace-level directory, but not in lower level subdirectories.

The path to the static library file is given by the value of the variable
$(rp_LIB). This is assigned a value \$(BLD_DIR)/rp/librp.a that gives
the absolute path to a file named librp.a in the rp subdirectory of the
relevant build directory.

The rule to construct the library constructs the target file \$(rp_LIB)
by archiving the contents of all of the object files in the src/rp
directory tree. The recipe of this rule variable \$(AR) to represent the
name of the archiver program that is used for this purpose. The value of AR
is defined in the config.mk file in the build directory, and is set to
"ar" by default.  The resulting library incorporates the contents of all
of the object files in the list \$(rp_OBJS).

The sources.mk file in every other namespace-level directory of src
defines an analogous rule for constructing a library file. A makefile
that includes a sources.mk file located in a namespace-level directory
has access to the object file list for that directory, the name of the
associated library file, and a rule to construct that library.

